<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=hypocaust">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Building and engineering</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hypocaust</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 80 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Also known as Hypocaustum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A hypocaust is a system of central heating in which hot air and smoke from a furnace circulate beneath a floor raised on stacks of tiles, the pilae, and rise through clay or tile flues in the walls. Vitruvius attributes its invention to Sergius Orata around 80 BCE, and described its construction in his De architectura of about 15 BCE. Hypocausts heated public baths and the villas of the wealthy throughout the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>, and their remains survive across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. The system fell out of use in the western provinces as the empire declined, but lived on in the Eastern Roman Empire and in the baths of Al Andalus.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hypocaust.jpg" alt="Hypocaust" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pascal RADIGUE · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pilettes_et_praefurnium_maison_grand_péristyle.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The word hypocaust comes from the Ancient Greek hupó, under, and kaustós, burnt. A system that produces and circulates hot air below the floor of a room, and may also warm the walls through a series of pipes, it could warm upper floors as well. The earliest reference to such a system suggests that the Temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a> was heated in this way in 350 BCE. Vitruvius attributes the invention to Sergius Orata, around 80 BCE. It improved the hygiene and living conditions of citizens and was a forerunner of modern central heating.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The floor of a hypocaust was raised above the ground on pillars called pilae stacks. These carried a layer of tiles, then a layer of concrete, then the floor tiles of the rooms above. Hot air and smoke from the furnace circulated through the enclosed space and rose through clay or tile flues in the walls, called caliducts, to outlets in the roof. This warmed the floors and walls above. The rooms meant to be warmest lay nearest the furnace, and the heat was regulated by the amount of wood fed to the fire.</p><p>Vitruvius describes its construction and operation in De architectura, around 15 BCE. He explains how fuel could be saved by placing the men's hot room, the caldarium, next to the women's, both beside the tepidarium, so that public baths ran efficiently. He also describes a bronze ventilator in the domed ceiling for adjusting the heat.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Hypocausts heated hot baths and other public buildings in ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, and also private homes. Wealthier merchants considered them proper and necessary for their villas across the empire. They were expensive and labour-intensive, since they needed constant attention to the fire and much fuel, and so were found mostly in large villas and public baths. Remains survive in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. In the Georgian settlement of Dzalisi, excavations in 1984-1985 uncovered a castle complex with a well-preserved hypocaust built between 200 and 400 CE. Korean ondol floor heating and the kang bed-stove of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> work on similar principles.</p><p>With the decline of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> the hypocaust fell into disuse in the western provinces, but not in the Eastern Roman Empire. Some monasteries used an evolution of it in warming rooms, or calefactories. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a> the Roman system was adopted for the Hispano-Islamic baths of Al Andalus, and a derivative, the gloria, was used in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> until modern heating arrived. In the 1720s the house of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland">Maryland</a> governor Charles Calvert was built with a hypocaust to heat a greenhouse for tropical plants.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hypocaust” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>c. 80 BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Italy</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Sergius Orata · <a href="/empire/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>The earliest reference suggests that the Temple of Ephesus was heated in this way in 350 BCE, and Garrett G. Fagan has disputed Orata’s priority.</p><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=hypocaust">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
